td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n$1$\n  </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186531631 1071 "A water tank with a capacity of $5,000$ liters is filled with water to $42.5%$ of its capacity. How much water is in the tank?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186531631 1071 "A water tank with a capacity of $5,000$ liters is filled with water to $42.5\\%$ of its capacity. How much water is in the tank?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186531631 1074 "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the capacity of the tank, which is $5,000$ liters. The percentage is $42.5%,$ and the part is the amount of water in the tank.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n42.5%=42.5÷100=0.425\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =42.5%\\times 5,000 \\\\  & =0.425\\times 5,000 \\\\  & =2,125\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $42.5%$ of $5,000$ is $2,125.$ Thus, there are $2,125$ liters of water in the tank.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n42.5%=\\frac{42.5}{100}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =42.5%\\times 5,000 \\\\  & =\\frac{42.5}{100}\\times 5,000 \\\\  & =42.5\\times \\frac{5,000}{100} \\\\  & =42.5\\times 50 \\\\  & =2,125\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $42.5%$ of $5,000$ is $2,125.$ Thus, there are $2,125$ liters of water in the tank." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186531631 1074 "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case,