17592186536817 1071 "Find a hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometric identity $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y).$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186536817 1074 "$$\nOsborn's rule allows us to construct a hyperbolic identity from a corresponding trigonometric identity.\n\nTo apply Osborn's rule, we follow two steps:\n\n1\\. Replace every trigonometric function with its corresponding hyperbolic function.\n\n2\\. For any product of two trigonometric sines, flip the sign of the corresponding product of hyperbolic sines.\n\nWe're given the following trigonometric identity:\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a product of sines. Therefore, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is \n\n$$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\,2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡y & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x-y)-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x+y) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡y & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x+y)-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x-y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186536817 1074 "$$\nOsborn's rule allows us to construct a hyperbolic identity from a corresponding trigonometric identity.\n\nTo apply Osborn's rule, we follow two steps:\n\n1\\. Replace every trigonometric function with its corresponding hyperbolic function.\n\n2\\. For any product of two trigonometric sines, flip the sign of the corresponding product of hyperbolic sines.\n\nWe're given the following trigonometric identity:\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a product of sines. Therefore, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\,2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡y & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x-y)-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x+y) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡y & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x+y)-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x-y).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186536824 1071 "What number is $175%$ greater than $48?$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186536824 1071 "What number is $175\\%$ greater than $48?$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186536824 1074 "To find a number $175%$ *greater than* $48,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%+175%=275%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n275%=\\frac{275}{100}=2.75\n$$\n\nSo, to find a number that is $175%$ greater than $48,$ we multiply $48$ by $2.75:$\n\n$$\n2.75\\cdot 48=132\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number that is $175%$ greater than $48$ is $132.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186536824 1074 "To find a number $175\\%$ *greater than* $48,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\%+175\\%=275\\%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n275\\%=\\frac{275}{100}=2.75\n$$\n\nSo, to find a number that is $175\\%$ greater than $48,$ we multiply $48$ by $2.75:$\n\n$$\n2.75\\cdot 48=132\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number that is $175\\%$ greater than $48$ is $132.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186536826 1071 "$$\n\n${\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x{csch}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186536826 1071 "$$\n{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x{csch}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186536826 1074 "$$\n\nFirst, we find the hyperbolic identities that correspond to the following trigonometric identities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identities are\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{coth}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{csch}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x{csch}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x})}^{2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{{sinh}^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ |\n\nNow, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{{sinh}^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3/4}^{4/3}\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3/4}^{4/3}{u}^{-3}\\,\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{-3+1}}{-3+1}\\,{|}_{3/4}^{4/3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{u}^{2}}\\,{|}_{3/4}^{4/3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{32}+\\frac{8}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{175}{288}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186536826 1074 "First, we find the hyperbolic identities that correspond to the following trigonometric identities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identities are\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{coth}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{csch}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x{csch}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x})}^{2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{{sinh}^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ |\n\nNow, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{{sinh}^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3/4}^{4/3}\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3/4}^{4/3}{u}^{-3}\\,\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{-3+1}}{-3+1}\\,{|}_{3/4}^{4/3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{u}^{2}}\\,{|}_{3/4}^{4/3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{32}+\\frac{8}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{175}{288}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186536840 1071 "$$\n\nEvaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/2)}^{0}\\frac{\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x}{{cosh}^{