}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{csch}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}{{csch}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{csch}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\cdot {sinh}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n\nNow, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{5/3}u\\,\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}{|}_{1}^{5/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({(\\frac{5}{3})}^{2}-{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{25}{9}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{9}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186537899 1071 "A company spends $C(x)=0.01{x}^{2}+4x+680$ dollars to produce $x$ units of a certain product. The company sells the product for $$12$ per unit. Given that the company can produce at most $500$ units per month, how many units should the company produce per month to maximize its monthly profit?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186537899 1071 "A company spends $C(x)=0.01{x}^{2}+4x+680$ dollars to produce $x$ units of a certain product. The company sells the product for $\\$12$ per unit. Given that the company can produce at most $500$ units per month, how many units should the company produce per month to maximize its monthly profit?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186537911 1071 "A factory spends $C(x)=100-47x+21{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}$ thousand dollars per month to manufacture $x$ units of a certain product. The factory sells the product for $$100\\,000$ per unit. Given that the factory can produce between $1$ and $10$ items per month, what is the maximum monthly profit it can make?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186537911 1071 "A factory spends $C(x)=100-47x+21{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}$ thousand dollars per month to manufacture $x$ units of a certain product. The factory sells the product for $\\$100\\,000$ per unit. Given that the factory can produce between $1$ and $10$ items per month, what is the maximum monthly profit it can make?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186537911 1074 "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ units of the product per month, the factory has a monthly revenue (income) of $R(x)=100\\cdot x=100x$ thousand dollars. \n\nTherefore, the profit function (in thousands of dollars) is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =100x-(100-47x+21{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-21{x}^{2}+147x-100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $1\\le x\\le 10.$ Therefore, $x=1$ and $x=10$ are both candidates for the global maxima of $P(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $1<x<10.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}-42x+147,\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-42x+147 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-14x+49 & =0 \\\\ (x-7)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check if $x=7$ gives a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=6x-42.\n$$\n\nEvaluating ${P}^{″}(x)$ at the critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{″}(7)=6(7)-42=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich does not confirm or deny that $x=7$ is a maximum of $P(x).$ Therefore, we should consider $x=7$ as one of the candidates.\n\nOur candidates are $x=1,$ $x=7$ and $x=10.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1) & =(1)^{3}-21(1)^{2}+147(1)-100=27 \\\\ P(7) & =(7)^{3}-21(7)^{2}+147(7)-100=243 \\\\ P(10) & =(10)^{3}-21(10)^{2}+147(10)-100=270\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum monthly profit is $270\\cdot $1\\,000=$270\\,000.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186537911 1074 "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ units of the product per month, the factory has a monthly revenue (income) of $R(x)=100\\cdot x=100x$ thousand dollars. \n\nTherefore, the profit function (in thousands of dollars) is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =100x-(100-47x+21{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-21{x}^{2}+147x-100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $1\\le x\\le 10.$ Therefore, $x=1$ and $x=10$ are both candidates for the global maxima of $P(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $1<x<10.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}-42x+147,\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-42x+147 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-14x+49 & =0 \\\\ (x-7)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check if $x=7$ gives a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=6x-42.\n$$\n\nEvaluating ${P}^{″}(x)$ at the critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{″}(7)=6(7)-42=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich does not confirm or deny that $x=7$ is a maximum of $P(x).$ Therefore, we should consider $x=7$ as one of the candidates.\n\nOur candidates are $x=1,$ $x=7$ and $x=10.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1) & =(1)^{3}-21(1)^{2}+147(1)-100=27 \\\\ P(7) & =(7)^{3}-21(7)^{2}+147(7)-100=243 \\\\ P(10) & =(10)^{3}-21(10)^{2}+147(10)-100=270\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum monthly profit is $270\\cdot \\$1\\,000=\\$270\\,000.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186537936 1071 "A tutoring center offers private lesson packages. When the price of a package is $$40,$ the center books all $80$ available sessions. For every $$4$ increase in the package price, one fewer session is booked. If the cost of providing each session is $$16,$ what price should the center charge to maximize its profit?\n\nTo maximize profit, the center should charge $$$\n{{field-1}}\nper session." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186537936 1071 "A tutoring center offers private lesson packages. When the price of a package is $\\$40,$ the center books all $80$ available sessions. For every $\\$4$ increase in the package price, one fewer session is booked. If the cost of providing each session is $\\$16,$ what price should the center charge to maximize its profit?\n\nTo maximize profit, the center should charge \\$\n{{field-1}}\nper session." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186537936 1074 "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nLet $x$ represent the number of unbooked sessions. Then the number of **booked** sessions is $(80-x),$ and the **price per session** is $(40+4x).$\n\nSo, the total revenue is\n\n$$\nR(x)=(80-x)(40+4x).\n$$\n\nExpanding, we get\n\n$$\nR(x)=-4{x}^{2}+280x+3200.\n$$\n\nThe cost of providing each booked session is $$16,$ so the total cost is\n\n$$\nC(x)=16(80-x)=1280-16x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =(-4{x}^{2}+280x+3200)-(1280-16x) \\\\  & =-4{x}^{2}+296x+1920.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given the domain $0\\le x\\le 80.$\n\nTo find the maximum, we take the derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=-8x+296.\n$$\n\nSolving ${P}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get:\n\n$$\nx=37.\n$$\n\nSince ${P}^{″}(x)=-8<0,$ this point is a maximum. Since $P(x)$ is quadratic, this maximum is global.\n\nNow, if $x=37$ sessions are unbooked, then the price is:\n\n$$\n40+4(37)=188.\n$$\n\nTherefore, to maximize profit, the center should charge $$\\,\\,188$ per session." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186537936 1074 "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nLet $x$ represent the number of unbooked sessions. Then the number of **booked** sessions is $(80-x),$ and the **price per session** is $(40+4x).$\n\nSo, the total revenue is\n\n$$\nR(x)=(80-x)(40+4x).\n$$\n\nExpanding, we get\n\n$$\nR(x)=-4{x}^{2}+280x+3200.\n$$\n\nThe cost of providing each booked session is $\\$16,$ so the total cost is\n\n$$\nC(x)=16(80-x)=1280-16x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =(-4{x}^{2}+280x+3200)-(1280-16x) \\\\  & =-4{x}^{2}+296x+1920.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given the domain $0\\le x\\le 80.$\n\nTo find the maximum, we take the derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=-8x+296.\n$$\n\nSolving ${P}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get:\n\n$$\nx=37.\n$$\n\nSince ${P}^{″}(x)=-8<0,$ this point is a maximum. Since $P(x)$ is quadratic, this maximum is global.\n\nNow, if $x=37$ sessions are unbooked, then the price is:\n\n$$\n40+4(37)=188.\n$$\n\nTherefore, to maximize profit, the center should charge $\\$\\,\\,188$ per session." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186537977 1071 "A daycare center can take care of between $10$ and $20$ children. The fee that parents must pay each month depends on the number of children enrolled in the center. If there are $10$ children enrolled, parents must pay $$40.$ On the other hand, if there are more than $10$ children enrolled, the price drops by $$2$ for each child. All the parents pay the same monthly fee.\n\nHow many children must enroll for the center to maximize its revenue?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186537977 1071 "A daycare center can take