peratorname{cosh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡8)}+1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(\\frac{8+{8}^{-1}}{2})}+1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(\\frac{65}{16})}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{49}{65}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186549057 1074 "Let's call $l$ the length of one of the legs. By the Pythagorean theorem, the length of the other leg is $\\sqrt{16-{l}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(l) & =4+l+\\sqrt{16-{l}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $l$ is the length of a leg of a right triangle whose hypotenuse is $4,$ the permitted range of $l$ is $0<l<4.$\n\nWe want to maximize $P(l),$ so we need to compute the critical points. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{-2l}{\\sqrt{16-{l}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{16-{l}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(l)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{16-{l}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{l}{\\sqrt{16-{l}^{2}}} & =1 \\\\ l & =\\sqrt{16-{l}^{2}} \\\\ {l}^{2} & =16-{l}^{2} \\\\ 2{l}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {l}^{2} & =8 \\\\ l & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $l=2\\sqrt{2}$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(l).$ To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n| Interval | $(0,2\\sqrt{2})$ | $2\\sqrt{2}$ | $(2\\sqrt{2},4)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ |  | $3$ |\n| ${P}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $P$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nSo, $l=2\\sqrt{2}$ does indeed give a maximum, and the perimeter of the resulting triangle will be the largest possible.\n\nTherefore, the greatest perimeter that a triangle can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2\\sqrt{2}) & =4+2\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{16-(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =4+2\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{16-8} \\\\  & =4+2\\sqrt{2}+2\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =4+4\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =4(1+\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [1759