.$ Hence,  $80$ should be a constant term.\n\nNow, if $h$ is the number of hours used to record an album, the cost  for $h$ hours of a recording must be $50h$ dollars.\n\nTherefore, the expression that represents the total cost, in dollars, of recording an album will be $50h+80.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186562391 1071 "What is the missing number?\n\n{{field-1}}\n$\\times \\,45%=18$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186562391 1071 "What is the missing number?\n\n{{field-1}}\n$\\times \\,45\\%=18$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186562391 1074 "The part is $18,$ the percentage is $45%,$ and we need to find the whole.\n\nTo do this, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{whole}=\\text{part}÷\\text{percentage}\n$$\n\nBefore we can substitute our numbers into the formula, we need to express $45%$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{45}{100}=\\frac{45÷5}{100÷5}=\\frac{9}{20}\n$$\n\nWe can now substitute our numbers into the formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{whole} & =18÷45% \\\\  & =18÷\\frac{9}{20} \\\\  & =18\\times \\frac{20}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{9}\\times 20 \\\\  & =2\\times 20 \\\\  & =40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $40.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186562391 1074 "The part is $18,$ the percentage is $45\\%,$ and we need to find the whole.\n\nTo do this, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{whole}=\\text{part}÷\\text{percentage}\n$$\n\nBefore we can substitute our numbers into the formula, we need to express $45\\%$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{45}{100}=\\frac{45÷5}{100÷5}=\\frac{9}{20}\n$$\n\nWe can now substitute our numbers into the formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{whole} & =18÷45\\% \\\\  & =18÷\\frac{9}{20} \\\\  & =18\\times \\frac{20}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{9}\\times 20 \\\\  & =2\\times 20 \\\\  & =40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $40.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186562474 1071 "A startup launched its website with $25$ initial visitors. An exponential model estimates that at the end of each day after launch, the number of visitors increased by $300%$ of the number of visitors at the end of the previous day.\n\nAccording to this model, how many visitors are there estimated to be after $3$ days?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186562474 1071 "A startup launched its website with $25$ initial visitors. An exponential model estimates that at the end of each day after launch, the number of visitors increased by $300\\%$ of the number of visitors at the end of the previous day.\n\nAccording to this model, how many visitors are there estimated to be after $3$ days?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186562474 1074 "The number of visitors $V(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $300%.$\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a=25$ is the total number of visitors initially\n\n- $r=3.0$ is the growth rate ($300%$ expressed as a decimal)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in days) after launch\n\nThe formula for exponential growth is\n\n$$\nV=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $V(t)$ is\n\n$$\nV=25\\cdot (1+3.0)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we get\n\n$$\nV(t)=25\\cdot (4)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTo find the total number of visitors after $3$ days, we evaluate the above for $t=3:$\n\n$$\nV(3)=25\\cdot (4)^{3}=25\\cdot 64=1600\n$$\n\nTherefore, the estimated number of visitors after $3$ days is $1600.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186562474 1074 "The number of visitors $V(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $300\\%.$\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a=25$ is the total number of visitors initially\n\n- $r=3.0$ is the growth rate ($300\\%$ expressed as a decimal)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in days) after launch\n\nThe formula for exponential growth is\n\n$$\nV=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $V(t)$ is\n\n$$\nV=25\\cdot (1+3.0)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we get\n\n$$\nV(t)=25\\cdot (4)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTo find the total number of visitors after $3$ days, we evaluate the above for $t=3:$\n\n$$\nV(3)=25\\cdot (4)^{3}=25\\cdot 64=1600\n$$\n\nTherefore, the estimated number of visitors after $3$ days is $1600.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186563688 1074 "From part 3, we have the function $p(x)=-6{x}^{2}+360x.$\n\nWe substitute $x=24$ into the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(24) & =-6(24)^{2}+360(24) \\\\  & =-3\\,456+8\\,640 \\\\  & =5\\,184\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the company will earn $$5\\,184.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186563688 1074 "From part 3, we have the function $p(x)=-6{x}^{2}+360x.$\n\nWe substitute $x=24$ into the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(24) & =-6(24)^{2}+360(24) \\\\  & =-3\\,456+8\\,640 \\\\  & =5\\,184\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the company will earn $\\$5\\,184.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186563759 1074 "From part 5, the required number of wooden tables $x$ is the solution to the equation\n\n$$\n6\\cdot ({x}^{2}-60x+800)=0.\n$$\n\nDividing this equation by $6,$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-60x+800=0.\n$$\n\nTo solve this equation, we want to find a factor pair of the constant term $(800)$ that also adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-60).$ The required factors are $(-20)$ and $(-40).$\n\nUsing this factor pair, we write the linear term as $-60x=-20x-40x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-60x+800 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-20x-40x+800 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-20x-40x+800 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-20x)+(-40x+800) & =0 \\\\ x(x-20)-40(x-20) & =0 \\\\ (x-20)(x-40)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the solutions are $x=20$ or $x=40.$ Since we require the minimum solution, we conclude that the company needs to produce $20$ wooden tables per week to make a profit of $$4\\,800.$" 1