n $49$ but less than $70$ will arrive early. Give your answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.7\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2420\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2389\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2358\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.6\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2743\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2709\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2676\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.5\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3085\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3050\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3015\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.08\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.09\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,2.9\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9985\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9986\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9986\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,3.0\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9989\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9990\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9990\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,3.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9992\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9993\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9993\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186583016 1071 "It is known that $24%$ of a given population have blood type A+. What is the probability that $3$ of $10$ randomly selected people have blood type A+? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186583016 1071 "It is known that $24\\%$ of a given population have blood type A+. What is the probability that $3$ of $10$ randomly selected people have blood type A+? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186583016 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of people having blood type A+.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=10$ people\n\n- the probability of having blood type A+ is $p=24%=0.24$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(10,0.24).$ We want to find the probability of having $x=3$ people with blood type A+.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{3})(0.24)^{3}(1-0.24)^{10-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{10!}{3!(10-3)!}(0.24)^{3}(0.76)^{7} \\\\  & =120(0.24)^{3}(0.76)^{7} \\\\  & =0.243\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186583016 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of people having blood type A+.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=10$ people\n\n- the probability of having blood type A+ is $p=24\\%=0.24$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(10,0.24).$ We want to find the probability of having $x=3$ people with blood type A+.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{3})(0.24)^{3}(1-0.24)^{10-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{10!}{3!(10-3)!}(0.24)^{3}(0.76)^{7} \\\\  & =120(0.24)^{3}(0.76)^{7} \\\\  & =0.243\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186583017 1071 "A seed packing company claims that the germination rate of the seeds is $75%.$ What is the probability that exactly $3$ seeds will germinate from $5$ seeds selected at random? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186583017 1071 "A seed packing company claims that the germination rate of the seeds is $75\\%.$ What is the probability that exactly $3$ seeds will germinate from $5$ seeds selected at random? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186583017 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of seeds that will germinate.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=5$ seeds\n\n- the probability of the germination of the seeds is $p=75%=0.75$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(5,0.75).$ We want to find the probability that exactly $3$ seeds will germinate.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{3})(0.75)^{3}(1-0.75)^{5-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5!}{3!(5-3)!}(0.75)^{3}(0.25)^{2} \\\\  & =10(0.75)^{3}(0.25)^{2} \\\\  & ≈0.264\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186583017 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of seeds that will germinate.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=5$ seeds\n\n- the probability of the germination of the seeds is $p=75\\%=0.75$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(5,0.75).$ We want to find the probability that exactly $3$ seeds will germinate.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{3})(0.75)^{3}(1-0.75)^{5-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5!}{3!(5-3)!}(0.75)^{3}(0.25)^{2} \\\\  & =10(0.75)^{3}(0.25)^{2} \\\\  & ≈0.264\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186583033 1071 "A student is randomly answering questions in a $10$-question math test. It is known that the student has a $25%$ probability of correctly answering each question. What is the probability that the student answers at most $1$ question correctly? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186583033 1071 "A student is randomly answering questions in a $10$-question math test. It is known that the student has a $25\\%$ probability of correctly answering each question. What is the probability that the student answers at most $1$ question correctly? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186583033 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of questions that the student answers correctly.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=10$ questions\n\n- the probability of answering each question correctly is $p=25%=0.25$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(10,0.25).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.25)^{x}(1-0.25)^{10-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.25)^{x}(0.75)^{10-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the student answers correctly at most $1$ question. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1) & =P(X=0)+P(X=1) \\\\  & =(0.75)^{10}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{1})(0.25)^{1}(0.75)^{10-1} \\\\  & =(0.75)^{10}+10(0.25)(0.75)^{9} \\\\  & =0.244\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186583033 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of questions that the student answers correctly.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=10$ questions\n\n- the probability of answering each question correctly is $p=25\\%=0.25$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(10,0.25).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.25)^{x}(1-0.25)^{10-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.25)^{x}(0.75)^{10-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the student answers correctly at most $1$ question. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1) & =P(X=0)+P(X=1) \\\\  & =(0.75)^{10}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{1})(0.25)^{1}(0.75)^{10-1} \\\\  & =(0.75)^{10}+10(0.25)(0.75)^{9} \\\\  & =0.244\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186583041 1071 "An unfair coin has a $20%$ chance of landing on heads. The coin is tossed $10$ times. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability of getting heads at least $3$ times?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186583041 1071 "An unfair coin has a $20\\%$ chance of landing on heads. The coin is tossed $10$ times. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability of getting heads at least $3$ times?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186583041 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times the coin landed on heads.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=10$ tosses.\n\n- The probability of landing on heads is $p=20%=0.2.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(10,0.2).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.2)^{x}(1-0.2)^{10-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.2)^{x}(0.8)^{10-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of getting heads at least $3$ times. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 3).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 3)=P(X=3)+P(X=4)+…+P(X=10)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 3) & =1-P(X\\le 2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)+P(X=2)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{0})(0.2)^{0}(0.8)^{10-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{1})(0.2)^{1}(0.8)^{10-1} \\\\  & \\,\\,+\\,(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{2})(0.2)^{2}(0.8)^{10-2}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.8)^{10}+10(0.2)(0.8)^{9}+45(0.2)^{2}(0.8)^{8}] \\\\  & ≈1-0.677\\,800 \\\\  & ≈0.322,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186583041 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times the coin landed on heads.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=10$ tosses.\n\n- The probability of landing on heads is $p=20\\%=0.2.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(10,0.2).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.2)^{x}(1-0.2)^{10-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.2)^{x}(0.8)^{10-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of getting heads at least $3$ times. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 3).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 3)=P(X=3)+P(X=4)+…+P(X=10)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 3) & =1-P(X\\le 2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)+P(X=2)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{0})(0.2)^{0}(0.8)^{10-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{1})(0.2)^{1}(0.8)^{10-1} \\\\  & \\,\\,+\\